---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import { SITE, DEFAULT_OG, fmtDate, iso, POSTS } from "../../lib/blog.mjs";

const slug = "landing-page-conversion-not-traffic";
const idx = POSTS.findIndex((p) => p.slug === slug);
const post = POSTS[idx];
const next = POSTS[(idx + 1) % POSTS.length];

const canonical = `${SITE}/blog/${slug}/`;
const ogImage = post.coverImage ? `${SITE}${post.coverImage}` : DEFAULT_OG;
const primaryTag = post.tags[0] || "Notes";
const readMins = post.readMins;
const showUpdated = post.updatedDate && +new Date(post.updatedDate) > +new Date(post.pubDate);
const updatedLabel = showUpdated ? `Updated ${fmtDate(post.updatedDate)}` : null;

const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      "@id": `${canonical}#post`,
      headline: post.title,
      description: post.description,
      datePublished: iso(post.pubDate),
      dateModified: iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate),
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Divyansh Sood", url: `${SITE}/`, "@id": `${SITE}/#person` },
      publisher: {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "@id": `${SITE}/#studio`,
        name: "Divyansh Sood® Studio",
        logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: `${SITE}/icon.svg` },
      },
      isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
      mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
      image: ogImage,
      articleSection: primaryTag,
      keywords: post.tags.join(", "),
      wordCount: 734,
      timeRequired: `PT${readMins}M`,
      speakable: {
        "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
        cssSelector: [".post-title", ".post-lead"],
      },
      inLanguage: "en",
      url: canonical,
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Blog", item: `${SITE}/blog/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: post.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
  ],
});
---

<Layout
  title={post.seoTitle ?? `${post.title} · Divyansh Sood`}
  description={post.description}
  canonical={canonical}
  keywords={post.tags.join(", ")}
  ogType="article"
  ogTitle={post.title}
  ogImage={ogImage}
  ogImageW={1200}
  ogImageH={750}
  articlePublished={iso(post.pubDate)}
  articleModified={iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate)}
  tags={post.tags}
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <Nav />
  <main>
    <header class="post-hero">
      <div class="post-hero-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          <span>{primaryTag}</span>
          <span>{fmtDate(post.pubDate)}</span>
          {updatedLabel && <span class="post-updated">{updatedLabel}</span>}
          <span>{readMins} min read</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="post-title">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">{post.description}</p>
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span>By Divyansh Sood</span><span>{fmtDate(post.pubDate)}</span><span>{readMins} min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    {post.coverImage && (
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <figure class="post-cover">
          <img src={post.coverImage} alt={post.title} width="1200" height="750" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    )}

    <article class="wrap narrow post-body">
      <p>When sales are slow, the instinct is to buy more traffic. More ads, more spend, more clicks.</p>
<p>But traffic is the most expensive lever you can pull, and usually the wrong one first. If your page converts at 1% and you double traffic, you’ve doubled your ad bill to keep the same leak. Fix the page to 2% and you’ve doubled revenue for free — and <em>then</em> the ads work twice as hard.</p>
<p>Here’s where pages actually leak, in the order they cost you the most.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="1-it-loads-too-slowly-to-matter">1. It loads too slowly to matter</h2>
<p>This is the leak nobody sees because it happens before anyone’s looking.</p>
<p>On mobile, a meaningful share of visitors abandon before a slow page even renders. They tapped your ad, waited two seconds, saw nothing, and left. You paid for that click and got a bounce.</p>
<p>A custom-built page should hit Lighthouse 90+ on mobile as a baseline. If yours is in the 50s — common on heavy builders and plugin-stacked sites — speed <em>is</em> your conversion problem, and no headline rewrite fixes it. Test yours at <a href="https://pagespeed.web.dev">pagespeed.web.dev</a> before you touch anything else.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="2-the-first-screen-doesnt-say-what-this-is">2. The first screen doesn’t say what this is</h2>
<p>You have about five seconds. In that window a visitor decides: <em>am I in the right place?</em></p>
<p>Most hero sections fail it. They lead with a clever tagline, a brand mood, or “Welcome to [company].” The visitor still doesn’t know what you do or whether it’s for them.</p>
<p>The hero needs three things, fast:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>What it is</strong> — in plain language, not a metaphor</li>
<li><strong>Who it’s for</strong> — so the right person feels seen</li>
<li><strong>One obvious next action</strong> — a single primary button</li>
</ul>
<p>If a stranger can’t tell what you offer from the first screen alone, the headline is the leak. Not the traffic.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="3-there-are-too-many-things-to-do">3. There are too many things to do</h2>
<p>Every link is an exit. A landing page with a full nav, five CTAs, social icons, and a newsletter popup is offering a dozen ways to leave and one way to convert.</p>
<p>Strong pages are ruthless. One primary action, repeated. Secondary paths demoted or removed. The more focused the page, the higher it converts — counterintuitive until you watch session recordings of people clicking everything <em>except</em> the button.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="4-the-call-to-action-creates-friction">4. The call-to-action creates friction</h2>
<p>People convert in the channel they already trust. For a huge range of businesses in India, that channel is WhatsApp — not a six-field form that emails an inbox nobody checks.</p>
<p>When we rebuilt <a href="https://www.divyanshsood.com/projects/nandini">Nandini Travels</a>, the unlock wasn’t traffic — they had plenty. It was giving every package a one-tap WhatsApp CTA with the details pre-filled. Bookings that used to leak to Google Maps started flowing through a site they own.</p>
<p>Match the CTA to how your customer actually wants to reach you. A frictionless WhatsApp tap beats a “perfect” form almost every time.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="5-theres-no-reason-to-believe-you">5. There’s no reason to believe you</h2>
<p>A page can be fast, clear, and focused — and still not convert, because the visitor has no reason to trust you yet.</p>
<p>Proof closes the gap: a real testimonial with a real name, a number you can stand behind, logos, a live result. <a href="https://www.divyanshsood.com/projects/modernkbs">Modern K.B.S.</a> converts on one line — “every other agency said 6 months; delivered in 7 days.” Specific, verifiable, and worth more than a paragraph of adjectives.</p>
<p>Vague claims (“trusted by many,” “industry-leading”) are invisible. Specific proof is what flips a maybe into a yes.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="the-order-that-actually-works">The order that actually works</h2>
<p>Before you raise the ad budget, run the page through this:</p>
<ul>
<li>✓ Lighthouse 90+ on mobile</li>
<li>✓ A stranger knows what you do from the first screen</li>
<li>✓ One primary action, distractions removed</li>
<li>✓ CTA in the channel your customer trusts (often WhatsApp)</li>
<li>✓ Specific, believable proof above the fold</li>
</ul>
<p>Fix the page first. Then every rupee you spend on traffic is worth roughly double — because you’re no longer paying to fill a bucket with a hole in it.</p>
<hr>
<p><em>I’m Divyansh Sood — I build landing pages that fix the leak before any extra ad spend goes in. 9 live builds since 2022. If your conversion rate is below 2% and you suspect the page is the bottleneck, not the traffic, <a href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com">email me</a> with your URL — I’ll tell you the three things I’d ship first.</em></p>
      <div class="tags">{post.tags.map((t) => <span>{t}</span>)}</div>
    </article>

    <section class="wrap narrow" aria-label="Keep reading">
      <a class="post-next" href={`/blog/${next.slug}/`}>
        <span><span class="label">Read next</span><br /><span class="name">{next.title}</span></span>
        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    <section class="post-foot-cta">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        {post.audience === "indian-smb" ? (
          <>
            <h2>Working on something in India?</h2>
            <p>If a post here matches what you're building, send me the brief. WhatsApp reply in under an hour, 9 AM–9 PM IST — I'll tell you within a day if it's a fit.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://wa.me/919816091875" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Message me on WhatsApp <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
            </div>
          </>
        ) : post.audience === "d2c-brand" ? (
          <>
            <h2>Building a brand a theme can't carry?</h2>
            <p>If your store has a model that off-the-shelf builders fight — hand-numbered drops, creator commerce, premium D2C — tell me about it. I read every brief personally.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com"
                >Email me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
            </div>
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <h2>Working on something similar?</h2>
            <p>If a post here matches what you're building, send me the brief. I reply personally, usually within a couple of hours — and I'll tell you within a day if it's a fit.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com"
                >Email me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://calendly.com/sood-divyansh007/30min" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Book a 15-min intro <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
            </div>
          </>
        )}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
